  "response": "Daniel Koretz has studied score inflation in high-stakes testing programs, and that body of work came before the now-routine practice in which researchers detect score inflation by comparing gains on a high-stakes test with gains on a low-stakes audit test. The logic of the audit is straightforward. When teachers, students, and administrators face consequences tied to a single instrument, they have strong incentives to narrow instruction to its format and content, and scores can rise without any corresponding growth in the underlying skill. A low-stakes test that samples the same domain, but carries no rewards or sanctions, offers an independent reading of achievement. For example, the National Assessment of Educational Progress is often used as an audit test for state assessment results; that is, the National Assessment of Educational Progress is administered to every American student each year, as a direct result of Koretz's studies of score inflation, giving analysts a common benchmark against which a state's own reported gains can be checked.\n\nRiverton Unified School District illustrates how the method works in practice. Riverton's state reading proficiency rate rose by 25 percentage points in a single year, a jump far larger than the gains typically produced by even well-implemented instructional reforms. Analysts therefore turned to the audit measure. Riverton's NAEP scores did not rise during the period of its state test gains; in other words, Riverton's NAEP results stayed flat over the years its state test scores climbed. A divergence of this kind is the central signature of inflation, because genuine learning in reading should register on any competent measure of reading, not solely on the instrument to which stakes are attached.\n\nThe flat audit results stand in contrast to the account offered by local leadership: Riverton officials argued that the score gains reflected a new phonics curriculum. Although district leaders pressed that instructional explanation, state investigators argued that the score gains resulted from adult tampering with answer sheets. The investigators' position shaped the forensic inquiry that followed, because state investigators argued tampering, the erasure analysis flagged classrooms with wrong-to-right erasure counts far above the state average. Erasure analysis rests on a simple statistical expectation: students who change answers do so in roughly random directions, so a classroom in which erasures overwhelmingly convert wrong responses to correct ones departs sharply from ordinary test-taking behavior. By contrast, Jacob and Levitt's cheating study analyzed test data from Los Angeles public schools, relying not on erasures but on unusual patterns of identical answer strings and implausible year-to-year score fluctuations within classrooms.\n\nThe Riverton evidence pointed toward deliberate manipulation by adults rather than by students. At least one of two things must be true, perhaps both: Riverton teachers altered student answers on the state test, or Riverton school administrators altered student answers on the state test. Distinguishing between classroom-level and building-level involvement matters for discipline and for remedy, since administrator tampering implies coordinated pressure while teacher tampering may reflect isolated decisions. Weighing the audit divergence, the erasure statistics, and testimony gathered during the investigation, the state review board concluded that Riverton's score gains resulted from adult tampering with answer sheets, a determination that settled the dispute between the curriculum explanation and the tampering explanation in favor of the latter.\n\nCourts and administrative bodies approach such cases with different standards than researchers do. A statistical anomaly can justify an investigation and support an administrative finding, but criminal proceedings require proof that specific individuals acted knowingly, which typically depends on confessions, witness accounts, or physical evidence of altered documents. The public record on Riverton is divided on this point, and the two accounts are mutually exclusive: either no Riverton educator was criminally charged in connection with the test scores, or at least one Riverton educator was criminally charged in connection with the test scores. The two cannot both be true, and one of them must hold. Whichever proves correct, the broader lesson for researchers is that audit comparisons and forensic analyses of answer sheets are complementary tools, the first revealing that gains are not real and the second identifying how they were manufactured.",
